{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J1 at head 00fed3ed, tree 912d909d, for request 7d6ce0f6 and review request a13e5f05. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. The route, the row access, the button and the witness hold. Five defects at the edges need fixing, and they change source, so the next head needs a new gate.\n\nAn independent reader, which I gave the code without my conclusions, read the routes and the page. I confirmed each item below at source. All of them come from reading the code, and none has run.\n\n## Changes I request\n\n1. A long `kind` hides every copy a member has. `requestOf` in `originals.ts` doesn't bound `intent.kind`, and `wellFormedText` has no length limit. `list` returns `kind` for each row. Two rows with a `kind` of about 600 KiB push the list past `BODY_BYTES + 4096`, the client throws, and `openOriginals` in `original-check.ts` returns `[]`. So every device of that member loses its lines, buttons and locks for seven days, and the page has no digest to end a row with. Only a key of that member can cause this, but revoking that key doesn't remove the rows. Bound `kind` at `keep`, and add a case.\n\n   Also, `openOriginals` treats a failed read as no copies. Its comment says that it blocks nothing, but it also clears the lock and hides the button. Make the page say that it couldn't read the open requests, and keep the fence.\n\n2. A full object stops every page act in the room, and removed members' rows keep counting. 64 members at 16 MiB reach the 1 GiB object cap, and each member's `keep` then answers `full`, including an admin's attempt to remove them. Removing a member leaves their rows counted and undeletable until the seven days pass, and no route clears them. I approved these caps in the design without seeing this, so this one is on my review as well. The room's own people are the actors here, so it's unlikely, but the failure takes the room's acts away for a week. Stop counting, or drop, the rows of a member or key no longer active, and don't tell a person with no open rows \"You have too many open requests\". Add a case.\n\n3. The page can say \"Nothing was sent\" with a row open, and that row can later send. In `data.ts`, `submitting?.()` runs after `keepOriginal`, and it throws on a room or key change. The row stays, reads \"You sent…\", locks the key, and a press past the margin sends the bytes. The scope normally refuses `expired`, but a device clock running ahead can land the act. Run the check before the keep, and end the row if anything stops the send after it. The same gap in the other direction is in `retained-editor-data.ts`: `check(options)` runs before `keepOriginal` and not again before `submit`, so a room or key change during that wait, up to 30 seconds, no longer stops the send. Add a case for each.\n\n4. A failed session renewal skips the copy without saying so. `fresh` sets `room.reader` to the failed answer's session, so `copiesOf` returns nothing and `keepOriginal` returns with no row. Then `act` sends. Section 9 says \"no session, no copy\" for a page on signed reads, but this page had a session a moment ago, and the person believes the act is covered. Choose: stop the send and say why, or send and say that this request keeps no copy. I prefer stopping, which matches your point 3. Add a case.\n\n5. Two statements aren't exact:\n   - `packages/contract/src/session.ts`, `sessions.ts` and `originals.ts` say a session \"cannot make or change\" a copy. That's your point 2, and the answer is no. `keep` takes any request whose signature verifies and whose actor is the session's key, and `texts`, `presented` and `values` aren't covered by the digest. So a holder can delete a row and keep it again with other unsigned bytes, or make a row from a request that key already signed and that hasn't expired. The scope verifies what travels beside the intent, and the signature still binds the act, so this grants nothing. Say that: it can make a copy only of a request that key signed, the unsigned parts can differ, and the scope verifies them when they're sent. Keep the contract edit to a comment.\n   - Section 9 says that connection requests keep their custody because \"their signer holds no session yet\". `issueInvitation` runs on a page that has read `room.me`, so that reason doesn't hold there. `issueDeviceKey` also calls `act` directly, which skips the lock and the fence. After a lost reply, the secret is gone, so a landed invitation has no link. State both as limits, or route them through the lock.\n\n## Your four points\n\n1. Yes, the fourth row still means what I approved. The claim is that the button adds no act and no signature to reach the answer. The window now closes at the scope's reply, and the store's check signs 32 random bytes with no domain and discards them. That check predates J1. The witness reports it apart as `keyChecksToLine`, with `domainToLine` empty, and the note says so.\n\n2. See item 5.\n\n3. Yes, \"nothing was sent\" is the right failure during a deployment's window. It's true, it can't lose a request, and it lasts minutes on one deployment. Tell the planner to expect it.\n\n4. Yes, a held request can hide an act, and I accept that bound. A row locks one key at one scope for at most `notAfter` plus the margin, so about six minutes for a page's five-minute intent. Other keys keep their acts. An opening act also locks the directory, so that key can't open anything new for that time. A lock on membership can delay that key's own `revoke-key`, and in that case a second admin or the recovery key still acts. A slow device clock extends it, and the way out is another device. Put that last limit in the note.\n\n## Not blocking\n\n- The button doesn't clear `lastActs` or the `sending` state, so the panel can still read \"outcome unknown\" after \"The request landed\" until a reload.\n- `originalAnswers[\"not-kept\"]` is never cleared.\n- A device link's `expected` revisions go stale after a later `enrol` or `invite-key` on that member, so a second outstanding link gets `revision-moved`. Name this in the note.\n- `/originals` answers 404 at a non-membership scope and 403 at a membership scope with no token, so it tells a caller with no token which kind a scope is.\n- \"Same bytes\" holds for rows the page wrote: the page parses and re-serialises the row. The witness's digest comparison covers that case.\n\n## What holds\n\n- Row access: `checkSession` binds the token to the scope and incarnation, standing reads at the head on every call, and an unknown digest and another member's digest both answer `not-found`.\n- The button signs no act. It verifies the signature, the digest and the actor, and a failed check sends nothing.\n- A row ends only on an answer. `unavailable`, a thrown error and an unverified receipt leave it open.\n- The caps sum in one synchronous call, so a race or a delete can't skew them.\n- `Wakes.set` takes the minimum across causes.\n- Text reaches the page through `h`, which uses attributes and text nodes. The reader found no `console` call in the page sources.\n\n## Your landing conditions\n\nAll five are met: the 24 named controls including the original four, the witness table, the measurement on loopback, the planner's ruling in section 9, and the kernel boundary check at 127 sources with 0 refusals.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The gate at 6e029473, tree f6b804b5, exits 0: Plain 3,242 with one skip, Native 916 with two skips. From there to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- From the witness and controls at b1537244 to that checkpoint, nothing under `packages/scope`, `packages/client` or `apps/room/page/src` changes.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` of 032e4f40 and this head gives this head's tree.\n- The evidence verifies. All 10 manifest rows match, the directory holds 137 files with none writable, and `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 3630da9c.\n- `witness/report.json` matches the note's table: 1 and 0 first-device acts, 0 and 1 second-device acts with the same SHA-256, no intent signature by the second device, and 0 open copies at the close. All 24 control rows read `distinguishes`. I read these and reran neither.\n- I ran `original-copy.scope.test.ts`, 9 of 9, and `security-entry-points.test.mjs`, 10 of 10. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` finds 780 links with 0 broken, `check-commit-messages` checks 12 with 0 flagged, and the worktree stayed clean.\n\nThe reader did not read `two-devices.mjs` or trace how the scope verifies stored `texts`, `presented` and `values`. No Worker, real room or second physical device has run this.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: the design holds as built. Approved in substance.\n- Security: not yet, for items 1 to 4. No item grants authority: they cost a person their buttons, their acts or a true message.\n- Simplification: no objection. `originals.ts` is one table and four routes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:983ab08ec99c9df139b03e88f14eec2f7c3d06b3","binding":"assigned","head":"00fed3ed65618fba06ab985242c14dd1d811c8fa","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6a19602ac492260e1dd35741538e10bd6eb8c5b4\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3649cf4ac25e995f657730259cbc8b1b415812d9\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:009e99006ed318a3793ade0de0b1e9f6a116e667","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}